<section>
  <title>Are you ready for more?</title>

  <section>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    <para>Mai wonders what would happen if we multiply equations. That is, we multiply the
      expressions on the left side of the two equations and set them equal to the expressions
      on the right side of the two equations.</para>
    <para>Use System B to answer questions 1 – 4.</para>
    <para>System B</para>
    <para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{c}2x+y=4\\x-y=11\end{array}\right.\)</para>

    <!--Start
      interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all
      have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the
      button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
    
          <para>Write out an equation that you would get if you multiply the two equations in this
            manner.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. \((2x+y)(x−y)=44\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>


      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>Does your original solution still work in this new equation?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Yes</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End
      interaction-->
      
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph your third equation
            and the System B equations using the Desmos tool below.</para>
            <media alt="">
              <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500"
              height="400" />
            </media>
          <para>When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
            button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>
            <figure class="unnumbered">
              <media alt="A system of equations.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_06_06_07.svg" />
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End
      interaction-->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>Explain if the approach of graphing the new equation is or is not particularly
            helpful.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>This approach is not helpful because the new equation is not a line and therefore
            not so easy to work with.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End
      interaction-->
    </section>
    </section>


    <section>
      <note class="self-check">
        <para>Solve the system of equations using elimination. Check the
        solution by graphing.</para>
        <para>
        \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;&amp;\left\{\begin{array}{l}2x\;–\;4y\;=\;–26\\-3x\;+\;4y\;=\;29\end{array}\right.\end{array}\)</para>
      </note>
    </section>

    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section>
        <title>Adding Systems of Equations and Using Their Graphs</title>
        <para>Look at the system of equations shown here:</para>
        <para>
          \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;&amp;\left\{\begin{array}{l}–3x\;+\;4y\;=\;7\\3x\;+\;2y\;=\;17\end{array}\right.\end{array}\)</para>
        <para>Let’s graph each equation on the coordinate plane. Identify the point of intersection.</para>
        <figure class="unnumbered">
          <media alt="GRAPH OF TWO LINES INTERSECTING AT THE POINT (3, 4).">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_06_08_01.jpg" />
          </media>
        
     
        <para>The lines intersect at \((3, 4)\).</para>
        <para>Add the two equations together. Simplify the resulting equation.</para>
        <para>\(\begin{array}{r}-3x+4y=7\\3x+2y=17\\\hline6y=24\\y=4\end{array}\)</para>
        <para>Graph the resulting equation on the same coordinate plane.</para>
        <figure class="unnumbered">
          <media alt="GRAPH OF THREE LINES INTERSECTING AT THE POINT (3,4).">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_06_08_02.jpg" />
          </media>
        

        <para>All three lines intersect at \((3, 4)\).</para>
        <para>You verified that by adding the two equations in the system, you created a third
          equation that is also true.</para>
        <para>The solution to the system is \(x = 3\), \(y = 4\).</para>
        <note class="try look-at-me">
        <title>Try It: Adding Systems of Equations and Using Their Graphs</title>
        <para>Add the equations to find the resulting equation. Then graph all three equations to
          find the ordered pair where they intersect.</para>
        <para>Use this system of equations to answer questions 1 – 4.</para>
        <para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{c}-2x-3y=7\\2x+y=3\end{array}\right.\)</para>
        <!--Q#1-->
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Add the equations to find the resulting equation.</para>

          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              \(\underline{\;\;\;\;\;-2x-3y=7\\+\;\;\;\;\;\;\;2x+y=3}\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;-2y\;=10\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;y\;=-5\\\)</para>
            <para>
              \(9\begin{array}{rcl}2x+\;y&amp;=&amp;3\;\\2x+\;(-5)&amp;=&amp;3\\2x&amp;=&amp;8\\x&amp;=&amp;4\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->

        <!--Q#2-->
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <!--QUESTION
            IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
            <para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph your third equation
              and the system of equations for this scenario using the Desmos tool below.</para>
          <media alt="">
